        {
            "title": "Inflammable Air from Acids",
            "summary": "Henry Cavendish trapped the gas that metals give off in acid and found that it burned. What the flame left behind was the real surprise.",
            "story": """Henry Cavendish preferred a quiet bench and a balance to a crowd. When metals sat in acid, a gas bubbled off, and he caught it instead of letting it drift away. A flame set the gas alight, so he called it inflammable air and measured how it behaved. He then burned a weighed portion and collected the product, expecting some sharp or sooty residue. What he found was water. His account in 1766 made inflammable air a substance in its own right, years before a classroom name was attached to the letter H.""",
        },
        {
            "title": "The Name Water-Former",
            "summary": "Antoine Lavoisier wanted chemical names to describe what substances actually did. A burning gas that produced water gave him a chance to prove the point.",
            "story": """Antoine Lavoisier was stripping old language out of chemistry and replacing it with names that reported facts. He knew the inflammable air from the English experiments, and he knew that burning it in oxygen produced water. He built the name hydrogen from Greek words meaning water-former. The name carried a claim: the gas was an element, and water was a compound, not an element itself. On the list of simple substances that he published, hydrogen had a place of its own. Students still write that choice every time they put an H at the start of a formula.""",
        },
